The $S$-$R$ interaction is a fundamental aspect of many-body systems. It is often described by the interaction Hamiltonian
\begin{equation}
H_{SR} = -J \sum_{i,j} \hat{S}_i \hat{R}_j + U \sum_{i} \langle i | i \rangle \langle R_j | j \rangle, \label{2h} 
\end{equation}
where $J$ is the strength of the direct interaction, $U$ is the exchange interaction, and $\hat{S}_i$ and $\hat{R}_j$ are the operators for the $i$-th spin $S$ and $R$ respectively. The $-J$ term represents the repulsion between spins, while the $U$ term describes the exchange interaction. The interaction is often considered to be weak, and the system is often treated as a many-body system with a large number of spins $N$.

