\section*{APPENDIX}

In the following, the qualitative behavior of the critical chemical potential $v = (\beta\mu)_c$ in the RG description of the homogeneous Bose gas is worked out. It appears quite difficult to do this directly from Eqs.\ (\ref{flow1}) and (\ref{flowtG}). However, these equations can be simplified in a way which, on the one hand, does not modify the essential behavior of $v$ but, on the other hand, allows to investigate the dependence on ${\hat a}\equiv a/\lambda_T$ analytically. In a first step, we use that typically $M\ll E_>$ with $E_>=1/2$ the scaled cutoff energy, and neglect the $M$ dependence of the Bose-Einstein distribution $N_{BE}$ and of the denominator $2(E_>-M)$ which appears in Eq.\ (\ref{flowtG}). Furthermore, we disregard the nontrivial renormalization of $\tilde G$ at high energies due to two-body scattering and thus drop the term $-\tilde G^2 b/2E_>$ from Eq.\ (\ref{flowtG}). The relation between the initial condition for $\tilde G(l)$ and the scattering length ${\hat a}$ is then given by $\tilde G(0) = 8\pi^2{\hat a} /\sqrt{2\pi b(0)}$. The flow equations now read
\begin{eqnarray}
\label{flow11}\frac{dM}{dl}&=&2M- 2\frac{{\widetilde G}}{2\pi^2}bN_{BE},\\
\label{flowtG1}\frac{d{\widetilde G}}{dl}&=& {\widetilde G}-\frac{{\widetilde G}^2b}{2\pi^2}\left[4bN_{BE}(N_{BE}+1)  +\frac{N_{BE}}{E_>}\right]
\end{eqnarray}
with $N_{BE}=[\exp(b(l)E_>)-1]^{-1}$. In a second step, Eq.\ (\ref{flowtG1}) is simplified further by setting 
$$b(l)N_{BE}=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc} 
0 & \quad b(l)\ge 1, \\
1/E_> & \quad b(l)< 1.
\end{array}\right. $$ This modification, which has only a small effect on the flow of $\tilde G$, turns  Eq.\ (\ref{flowtG1}) into
\begin{equation}\label{flowG3}
\frac{d{\widetilde G}}{dl}=\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
{\widetilde G} & \quad l\le \ln \sqrt{b(0)}, \\
{\widetilde G} -q {\widetilde G}^2 & \quad l > \ln\sqrt{b(0)}
\end{array}\right.
\end{equation}
where $q=20/2\pi^2$.

samples.second_piece:
The solution to this equation is given by
\begin{equation}\label{solG}
{\widetilde G}(l) =\left\{ \begin{array}{cc}
{\widetilde G}(0)e^l & 
l\le \ln \sqrt{b(0)}, \\[0.1cm]
{\displaystyle \frac{{\widetilde G}(0) e^l}{1+q {\widetilde G}(0) [e^l-\sqrt{b(0)}]} } & 
l > \ln\sqrt{b(0)}.
\end{array}\right.
\end{equation}
The critical initial condition $M_c(0)$ for the chemical potential is determined by the condition $e^{-2l}M(l\to\infty) = 0$. From Eq.\ (\ref{flow11}) thus follows that
\begin{eqnarray}
M_c(0) &=&\frac 1{2\pi^2} \int_0^\infty 2\,{\widetilde G}(l) b(l)N_{BE} e^{-2l} dl \nonumber \\
&=&\frac 1{2\pi^2}\int_0^\infty  2\,{\widetilde G}(0) e^l b(l)N_{BE} e^{-2l} dl\nonumber \\
&& + \frac 1{2\pi^2}\int_{\ln \sqrt{b(0)}}^\infty 2\left(\frac{{\widetilde G}(0) e^l}{1+q {\widetilde G}(0) (e^l-\sqrt{b(0)})}\right. \nonumber \\
&& \hspace*{0.8cm} - {\widetilde G}(0)e^l \bigg)b(l)N_{BE} e^{-2l} dl\nonumber \\
&\equiv& I_1 +I_2.
\end{eqnarray}
The first integral $I_1$ equals $b(0)^{-1} 4\zeta(3/2) {\hat a}$, whereas $I_2$ can be rewritten as
\begin{equation}\label{expansion}
I_2= \frac{q{\widetilde G}(0)^2}{2\pi^2} \int_0^1 dx \frac{\sqrt x -1}{1-q{\widetilde G}(0)\sqrt{b(0)}(1-1/\sqrt x)} \frac 1 {e^{xE_>}-1}. 
\end{equation}
Expanding the second factor in the integrand as $1/(e^{xE_>}-1) = 1/xE_> -1/2 +\cdots$, one sees that expression (\ref{expansion}) can be written as
\begin{equation}
I_2= \frac 1{b(0)} \left[\frac{640}\pi {\hat a}^2\ln {\hat a} + b_2'' {\hat a}^2 +O({\hat a}^3)\right].
\end{equation}
It is interesting to note that, similarly to the effective-field theory calculation of Ref.\ \cite{ArnTom01}, only the coefficient of ${\hat a}^2\ln {\hat a}$ can be determined by the perturbation expansion. All other terms apparently receive contributions from all expansion orders and thus cannot be calculated in this way. Putting together the above results and using numerical data to obtain the coefficient of the ${\hat a}^2$ term, we finally find
\begin{equation}
v=(\beta\mu)_c = 4\zeta(3/2) {\hat a} + \frac{640}\pi {\hat a}^2\ln {\hat a} + 908 {\hat a}^2 +O({\hat a}^3)
\end{equation}
in the simplified approach studied here. It should be remarked that even in this simple model, the term linear in ${\hat a}$ has the correct prefactor, whereas the coefficients of the two second-order terms differ by only a factor of about 2 from the values given in ref.\ \cite{ArnTom01} and the results of our original RG equations. We also note that from the numerical simulation of Eqs.\ (\ref{flow11}) and (\ref{flowG3}) we can accurately reproduce the coefficient $640/\pi$ of the ${\hat a}^2\ln {\hat a}$ term, thus confirming the accuracy of the fitting procedure described in the main text.

