\section{Stability of the normal modes}
\label{Stab}
The stability of the normal modes is crucial for understanding the dynamics of the fluid. In the linearized approximation, the stability of the normal mode solutions is determined by the eigenvalues of the linearized operator. Specifically, the dominant eigenvalue of the linearized operator determines the growth rate of perturbations in the fluid. For the normal modes derived in the first section, we analyze the eigenvalues of the linearized operator associated with the equation \eqref{NS-eq}. The dominant eigenvalue is found to be \(\lambda_{\text{dom}} = 0\), indicating that the normal modes are stable in the linear approximation.

The stability of the normal modes is also reflected in the absence of significant oscillations in the pressure field when small perturbations in the fluid state are considered. This is consistent with the fact that the pressure is a conserved quantity and does not exhibit significant fluctuations in the absence of external forces or changes in density.

In summary, the stability of the normal modes is a key aspect of the fluid dynamics described by the normal modes derived in this section. It ensures that the fluid behaves predictably and consistently under small perturbations.

