\section{Introduction}

General transfer (response) functions for LISA-like missions has been studied in much detail in the past (see, e.g. \cite{Cornish:2002rt}). Here we adapt the results to the particular case of TianQin and present the detailed form of the transfer function used in our calculations \cite{TQ}, assuming that the source is a binary system with a circular orbit.

\section{Gravitational waves from a binary source}

To specify the orientation of the binary orbit and the relative direction between the detector and the source, we follow \cite{Poisson2014} and set up the coordinate systems as in Fig. \ref{fig.binary}. For a binary source with a circular orbit, the gravitational waves can be written as
\begin{eqnarray} h_+&=&h_0a_+e^{2iwt} +c.c.\,,\quad a_+=-\Big[ \frac12(1+c_\delta^2) c_{2\gamma}+ic_\delta s_{2\gamma}\Big] e^{2i\beta}\,,\nonumber\\
h_\times&=&h_0a_\times e^{2iwt}+c.c.\,,\quad a_\times=-\Big[\frac12 (1+c_\delta^2) s_{2\gamma} -ic_\delta c_{2\gamma} \Big] e^{2i\beta}\,,\label{h.J0806}\end{eqnarray}
